<h2>The case</h2>
<p>Karimpur P.S. Case No. 305 of 2026 was registered on 13 September 2026, invoking Sections 302, 406, 417, 506 and 34 of the Indian Penal Code as recorded in the order. The striking feature was chronology: the FIR was lodged about seven and a half years after the events it narrates, and, as the Court noted, no explanation was offered for the delay. The petitioners&rsquo; counsel contended they were falsely implicated; the State opposed the plea after producing the case diary.</p>
<p>A further feature weighed in: the principal accused had already received the benefit of Section 482 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita — the provision that now houses anticipatory bail. Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Partha Sarathi Chatterjee, considering the petitioners&rsquo; alleged role alongside the stale and unexplained FIR, extended the same protection.</p>

<h2>The order and its conditions</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Relief.</strong> Anticipatory bail granted: in the event of arrest, release on a bond of Rs. 20,000 with two sureties of like amount each, one of whom must be local.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Cooperation.</strong> The petitioners must cooperate with the investigation and meet the investigating officer as and when called.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Integrity of trial.</strong> No threats or intimidation of witnesses; no tampering with evidence.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Disposal.</strong> The application stands disposed of on those terms.</div>
</div>

<h2>Stale FIRs and the logic of pre-arrest protection</h2>
<p>Three strands of reasoning converge in orders of this kind. First, the purpose of arrest: custodial interrogation is justified by investigative need, and a complaint that waited years for registration rarely presents the urgency that custody serves. Second, the quality of the accusation: unexplained delay is a circumstance the trial court will weigh, and at the bail stage it softens the prima facie picture. Third, parity: the criminal law should not produce the spectacle of the alleged principal walking protected while alleged associates face arrest on the same, aged allegations.</p>
<p>None of this forecloses the investigation. Anticipatory bail orders of the Calcutta High Court in this format are working arrangements: the investigation proceeds, the accused attends, and the safeguards against witness interference stand as enforceable conditions whose breach invites cancellation.</p>

<h2>Points for practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Build the chronology exhibit first: date of alleged occurrence, date of complaint, date of FIR. In stale-FIR cases the timeline is the argument.</p>
  <p>Put the absence of explanation on record — if the complaint itself offers none, say so; if it offers one, meet it.</p>
  <p>Track co-accused orders and annex them. Parity arguments succeed on documents, not assertions.</p>
  <p>Advise clients that conditions are not decorative: a missed appearance before the investigating officer is the fastest route to cancellation.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information and does not comment on any pending investigation or trial.</div>
